# probe-subscriber-ceiling — is the per-subscriber delivery ceiling the BROKER
# or the load generator? (ADR 0077 T7)
#
# Not an industry shape. A diagnostic, and the cheapest measurement in ADR 0077
# whose answer changes an architecture before it is built.
#
# THE CLAIM UNDER TEST. bench/scale/workloads/README.md records a ceiling of
# ~1000 msg/s per subscriber, seen across publisher counts 3000/4000/6000, both
# emqtt-bench socket modes, and offers from 200k to 800k: delivery landed at
# 300-326k against 300 subscribers every time. That number decides how many
# datalake consumers a SCADA estate needs — at 30 000 msg/s per site it is the
# difference between 2 consumers per site and 30, and across 3 000 sites the
# difference between ~6 000 writers and 90 000.
#
# WHY IT IS SUSPECT. Every $share measurement this rig holds is QoS 1, where a
# delivery costs a PUBACK round trip and is gated by receive-maximum. Every QoS 0
# measurement is BROADCAST fan-out, not $share — and there a single subscriber
# sustained 10 806 msg/s, while the 240-subscriber runs never plateaued at all
# (1 201 -> 2 403 -> 4 123 msg/s per subscriber as offered load rose, which is
# the OFFER increasing, not a ceiling being found).
#
# So the ~1000 is very likely a QoS 1 artifact. "Very likely" is not a number,
# and extrapolating across a mechanism boundary — fan-out to $share — is exactly
# the step that produced wrong answers throughout the 2026-08 campaign.
#
# THE DESIGN. Hold the group at 10 subscribers and sweep the OFFERED rate, so
# the per-subscriber rate is the rung divided by ten:
#
#     200 000 offered -> 20 000 per subscriber
#     100 000         -> 10 000
#      50 000         ->  5 000
#      20 000         ->  2 000
#
# Sweeping the rate rather than the group size keeps this to ONE cluster
# provisioning, because rate is what lane B's rungs already vary.
#
# Read it as: the highest rung whose DELIVERED rate still tracks its offered rate,
# divided by 10. If delivery flattens near 10 000 total, the ceiling is real and
# the SCADA design needs ~30 consumers per site. If it tracks to 100 000 or
# 200 000, the ceiling was QoS 1's and 2-4 per site is right.
#
# Run this file TWICE — once as-is, once with LANE_B_QOS=1 in the environment —
# because the existing QoS 1 figure comes from a different shape (420 subscribers,
# 100 000 publishers, and a run that was cap-limited), so it is not a fair
# comparison. Same shape, one variable.
LANES=B
LANE_B_QOS=0
LANE_B_FANOUT=0

# 2000 publishers is the smallest population for which every rung above needs an
# exact integer -I of at least 6ms (2 000 000 / 200 000 = 10ms at the top).
LANE_B_PUBS_OVERRIDE=2000
LANE_B_SUBS_OVERRIDE=10
LANE_B_PUB_CONTAINERS=5
LANE_B_SUB_CONTAINERS=1

LANE_B_RUNGS_OVERRIDE="200000 100000 50000 20000"
LANE_B_REF_RUNG=50000
